Z||000000||gkozlov||09-06-2000||05:47 PM||||A. I cant seem to get my interior door panel lined up. Its all lined up except for the top. <P>B. Im also having a problem with my manual mirror thingy sticking up. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/door.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||09-06-2000||06:17 PM||||Your int. door panel is warped due to sun/heat. Pull it and get another from a junkyard.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gkozlov||09-06-2000||06:27 PM||||Is there anyway to fix it without replacing it?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">   <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A><p>[This message has been edited by gkozlov (edited 09-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||286SE'S||09-06-2000||07:17 PM||||Be sure that the top fasteners (christmas trees) on the panel are holding  securely. All the auto parts stores have the GM ones, Take an old one with you to match it up. I had to superglue the top of my panel into the black channel otherwise it popped out whenever the door was closed. The glue only held for a year. That remote looks just like mine, sticks up. It is held in by nuts that thread on to the studs comming out of the joystick frame but in my opinion should have had a brace on the cardboard panel to hold it better. Something I have considered is making something out of some thin steel to flatten out the door panels weak fiberboard. Cut out a square with a hole for the studs and cable and place it  under the door panel. Tighten the nuts and it should flatten out. <BR>Note: There is one allen screw visible on the joystick from the outside with the window down. loosening it allows the joystick with cable to drop out. Hope this helps.<BR>Terry||||reg||10
Z||000004||gkozlov||09-06-2000||09:38 PM||||Thanks. I'll go to the auto parts store and buy the "christmas trees" I'll replace all of them regardless. Ive put alot of time and effert into the interior of my car. Its that one door panel giving me all the problems.<BR>Maybe I can use this as an excuse to get power mirrors. My wife might go for it. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||09-06-2000||09:52 PM||||If you get power mirrors (and want your interior to stay looking good) you need to do one of two things.  Either replace the door panel with one meant for p/mirrors (no hole on top) or find a replacement joystick type p/mirror control (in which case you'll have to figure out how to wire it up.  I bought a mirror control unit from an Escort at the local boneyard, but couldn't figure out how to wire it up and didn't use it.  The Grape Ape came with p/mirrors so it's just sitting in the garage collecting dust.  If you chose to go this route (be prepared to trace a bunch of wiring) LMK and you can have the Escort mirror control for the price of shipping.||||reg||1
Z||000006||gkozlov||09-06-2000||09:58 PM||||Maybe power windows arent the answer for me either. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-06-2000||11:41 PM||||Take the panel off and try putting washers under the mirror trim's nuts. Use as large a washer as you can get on it. You want "fender" washers, small centers and big outside. That will spread the load and should help it stay put. (You might have to make something out of light sheet stock.)<P>As for that warp, It's bowed in the middle so the fasteners likely aren't the problem. You'll need to glue something to the bottom of that top section. No, I'm not sure exactly how you'd go about it. A strip of flat aluminum or steel might work. Needs to be thin and very stiff. If you pull out a bit of insulation maybe theres room for some light angle stock. <P>These pannels are esentially glorified card board with stuff glued to it. They aren't as rigid as they really need to be. Finding good ones ain't easy.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||gkozlov||09-06-2000||11:46 PM||||Thanks. I'll try it. <P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||80's BOY||09-07-2000||11:23 PM||||there's also a large metal groove on the top of the door. your door panel slides in this upon installation. i had to spread mine apart with a screwdriver, push the panel in it, hold it there and tap it in place with a rubber mallet. it sort-of "pinches" down and grabs the panel to hold it. could be worth a try in your case. good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000010||gkozlov||10-02-2000||06:57 AM||||Thanks to all who tried to help. <BR>I have finally figured it out. I put the door panel on incorrectly. As far as the mirror adjustment thingy goes- you must route the cable up. The door its self was just as 286SE'S mentioned. The Xmas trees were broken and bent. I will take pic and post it later of the fixed product. This is the greatest resource for any Fiero owner. Maybe one day Ill know what the heck Im talking about when it comes to these cars and be able to help. Thanks.  <P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">   <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A><p>[This message has been edited by gkozlov (edited 10-02-2000).]||||reg||1
